QB.add(14,[{"n":1,"q":"A 50-year-old woman visits her physician for a routine check-up. Her laboratory results reveal a fasting plasma glucose level of 104 mg/dL (5.8 mmol/L) and an HbA1c level of 44 mmol/mol (6.2%). Which of the following statements about her condition is correct?","o":[{"t":"She is diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus."},{"t":"She meets the criteria for pre-diabetes based on her fasting plasma glucose level."},{"t":"Her HbA1c level indicates normal glucose metabolism."},{"t":"She requires immediate insulin therapy."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's fasting plasma glucose of 104 mg/dL is above the normal range and falls within the category of impaired fasting glucose (‡ 100 mg/dL) , indicating a pre-diabetic state."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Additionally, her HbA1c level of 44 mmol/mol (approximately 6.2%) supports the diagnosis of pre-diabetes rather than normal glucose metabolism."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pre-diabetes:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pre-diabetes involves elevated blood glucose levels that are not high enough for a type 2 diabetes diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Also referred to as: Impaired Glucose Tolerance (IGT) Impaired Fasting Glucose (IFG)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Impaired Glucose Tolerance (IGT)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Impaired Fasting Glucose (IFG)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Impaired Glucose Tolerance (IGT)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Impaired Fasting Glucose (IFG)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Difference between IFG and IGT"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Criteria Impaired Fasting Glucose (IFG) Impaired Glucose Tolerance (IGT)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Indicates Poor glucose regulation after fasting Impaired insulin sensitivity or"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"inadequate insulin production after sugar intake"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology: IFG suggests early insulin IGT indicates a more pronounced"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"resistance or beta-cell dysfunction. glucose metabolism impairment."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic Criteria for Pre-diabetes (Option B)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Criterion Definition"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Impaired Fasting Glucose Fasting plasma glucose: ‡ 6.1 mmol/L (110 mg/dL) < 7.0 (IFG) mmol/L (126 mg/dL) (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"The American Diabetes Association suggests a lower limit of ‡ 100 mg/dL (5.6 mmol/L) ."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Criterion Definition"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Impaired Glucose Tolerance Fasting plasma glucose: < 126 mg/dL (7.0 mmol/L) (IGT) 2-hour glucose after 75 g oral glucose drink: 140–200"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"mg/dL (7.8–11.1 mmol/L)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"HbA1c Criteria NICE guidelines: 6.0% to 6.4% (42–47 mmol/mol) (Option C ruled out) American Diabetes Association: 5.7% to 6.4% (39–47"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"mmol/mol)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Significance of Pre-diabetes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased Diabetes Risk : Higher likelihood of developing type 2 diabetes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cardiovascular Disease Risk : Linked to macrovascular issues (e.g., atherosclerosis), increasing risks of: Myocardial infarction Stroke Peripheral vascular disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myocardial infarction"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stroke"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peripheral vascular disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myocardial infarction"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stroke"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peripheral vascular disease"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of Pre-diabetes (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management Strategy Details"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lifestyle Modifications Weight Management : Healthy weight through diet and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"exercise. Healthy Eating : Balanced diet low in saturated fat, cholesterol, and sugars; high in fiber, fruits, and vegetables. Physical Activity : At least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity aerobic exercise weekly."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cardiovascular Risk Aggressive management of hypertension and Management dyslipidemia to reduce cardiovascular risks."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Medication Consider metformin to delay progression to type 2"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"diabetes, if necessary."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Screening for Pre-diabetes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Regular screening is advised for individuals with risk factors such as: Obesity Family history of diabetes Certain ethnic backgrounds History of gestational diabetes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Obesity"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Family history of diabetes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Certain ethnic backgrounds"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"History of gestational diabetes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Obesity"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Family history of diabetes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Certain ethnic backgrounds"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"History of gestational diabetes"}]},{"n":2,"q":"Which of the following is most characteristic of Type 3 Diabetes (T3DM)?","o":[{"t":"Insulin resistance affecting the muscles and fat tissue"},{"t":"Primarily affects cognitive functions like memory due to insulin resistance in the brain"},{"t":"No connection to Alzheimer's disease"},{"t":"Treated solely through oral hypoglycemic agents"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This defines T3DM, where insulin resistance affects neurons, leading to cognitive decline and neurodegenerative processes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type 3 Diabetes (T3DM):"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Definition: A term primarily linked with Alzheimer's disease (AD), describing insulin resistance and deficiency affecting the brain. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Characteristics of Type 3 Diabetes:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Characteristic Details"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Insulin Resistance Neurons cannot effectively respond to insulin, impairing in Brain cognitive functions like memory."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Connection to AD AD is associated with impaired insulin signalling, leading"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"some to label it as \"diabetes of the brain.\""},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathophysiology Impaired insulin signalling leads to amyloid-beta"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"plaques, tau tangles, oxidative stress, and neuroinflammation, contributing to neurodegeneration and cognitive decline."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Implications for Treatment:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Approach Details (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Insulin Sensitizers Medications improving insulin sensitivity could have"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"neuroprotective effects."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Approach Details (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lifestyle Interventions Diet and exercise targeting metabolic health may slow"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"AD progression and benefit cognition."}]},{"n":3,"q":"Which of the following is most characteristic of Latent Autoimmune Diabetes in Adulthood (LADA)?","o":[{"t":"Rapid b-cell destruction similar to type 1 diabetes"},{"t":"Absence of islet autoantibodies"},{"t":"Gradual b-cell destruction and eventual insulin requirement"},{"t":"D) Complete management through lifestyle modifications and oral antidiabetic agents"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"LADA progresses slowly, but patients eventually require insulin as their b-cell function declines."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Latent Autoimmune Diabetes in Adulthood (LADA):"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Definition: A form of diabetes that shares features of both type 1 and type 2 diabetes, with a slower onset, usually diagnosed in adulthood."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Key Characteristics:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Characteristic Details"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Autoimmune Basis Presence of islet autoantibodies (e.g., GAD antibodies)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"indicating autoimmune b-cell destruction."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Slower Progression Gradual b-cell destruction; patients may not initially"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"require insulin but will eventually need it."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Diagnostic Challeng Often confused with type 2 diabetes; islet autoantibodies e help in distinguishing it."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Key Considerations for LADA:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Consideration Details"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Early Identification Timely recognition is essential for preserving b-cell"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"function with early insulin initiation."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Strategie Initially managed like type 2 diabetes (lifestyle changes, s oral medications); insulin therapy needed later."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Monitoring and Regular monitoring (glucose, HbA1c, islet Education autoantibodies) and patient education on"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"self-management are crucial."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Rapid b-cell destruction similar to type 1 diabetes (Option A): LADA progresses more slowly compared to classic type 1 diabetes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Absence of islet autoantibodies (Option B): Islet autoantibodies, particularly GAD antibodies, are present in LADA, indicating an autoimmune process."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Complete management through lifestyle modifications and oral antidiabetic agents (Option D): While lifestyle modifications and oral agents may help initially, insulin therapy is eventually needed."}]},{"n":4,"q":"Which of the following is required to diagnose complete remission of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (T2DM)?","o":[{"t":"HbA1c below 6.5% and fasting glucose between 100 and 124 mg/dL without treatment"},{"t":"HbA1c below 6.0% and fasting glucose below 100 mg/dL sustained without treatment"},{"t":"HbA1c below 7.0% and fasting glucose below 110 mg/dL with lifestyle modifications"},{"t":"D) HbA1c below 6.5% for at least six months without medication"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"HbA1c below 6.0% and fasting glucose below 100 mg/dL without treatment defines complete remission, where normal blood glucose levels are maintained without medication."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Remission of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (T2DM)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Definition of Remission:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Partial Remission: HbA1c < 6.5% (48 mmol/mol) and fasting glucose 100 - 124 mg/dL for at least one year without medication. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Complete Remission: HbA1c < 6.0% (42 mmol/mol) and fasting glucose < 100 mg/dL sustained without treatment."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Factors Contributing to Remission:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weight Loss: Significant weight loss (e.g., 15% body weight) leads to remission in many patients. DiRECT trial: 85% of participants achieving significant weight loss entered remission."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Significant weight loss (e.g., 15% body weight) leads to remission in many patients."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DiRECT trial: 85% of participants achieving significant weight loss entered remission."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bariatric Surgery: Results in major weight loss (up to 40 kg) with remission rates of 70–80%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Results in major weight loss (up to 40 kg) with remission rates of 70–80%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Medications: Glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1 RAs) aid in sustained weight loss, potentially leading to remission."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1 RAs) aid in sustained weight loss, potentially leading to remission."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Significant weight loss (e.g., 15% body weight) leads to remission in many patients."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DiRECT trial: 85% of participants achieving significant weight loss entered remission."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Results in major weight loss (up to 40 kg) with remission rates of 70–80%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1 RAs) aid in sustained weight loss, potentially leading to remission."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Implications: Lifestyle modifications, bariatric surgery, and advanced medications shift the focus towards achieving remission, rather than merely managing T2DM symptoms."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"HbA1c below 7.0% and fasting glucose below 110 mg/dL with lifestyle modifications (Option C): These levels do not meet the criteria for complete remission, and the requirement for lifestyle modifications indicates ongoing management."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"HbA1c below 6.5% for at least six months without medication (Option D): The timeframe is insufficient, as complete remission requires at least one year without treatment."}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 25-year-old male with thalassemia major presents to the clinic with increased fatigue, joint pain, and symptoms of hyperglycemia. On examination, he has a discoloration of the skin as shown. His medical history includes multiple blood transfusions over the years. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial step in managing his condition?","o":[{"t":"Initiate a low-carbohydrate diet"},{"t":"B) Start iron chelation therapy"},{"t":"Begin insulin therapy"},{"t":"D) Schedule a follow-up for skin assessment"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient has Bronze Diabetes, a form of diabetes associated with iron overload due to repeated blood transfusions in thalassemia major. This condition leads to impaired insulin secretion, resulting in hyperglycemia and characteristic skin changes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bronze Diabetes:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Definition: A form of diabetes associated with iron overload, commonly seen in patients with thalassemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Characteristics: Named for the characteristic greyish skin discoloration due to excess iron deposits (hemosiderin staining)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Underlying Mechanism: Caused by repeated blood transfusions in thalassemia management, leading to significant iron accumulation over time."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Features of Bronze Diabetes:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Feature Details"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Cause Primarily linked to thalassemia major"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Regular blood transfusions cause pancreatic iron overload, impairing insulin secretion"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Symptoms Typical diabetes symptoms (hyperglycemia)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Joint pain and fatigue Skin changes less prominent in darker complexions"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Feature Details"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Diagnosis Often after diabetic ketoacidosis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Elevated blood glucose levels and serum ferritin indicating iron overload"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management Details"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Insulin Therapy Many patients require insulin for effective blood glucose"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"management."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Iron Chelation Helps reduce body iron levels, mitigating complications Therapy from iron overload."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Regular Monitoring Continuous assessment of blood glucose and serum"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ferritin is crucial to prevent complications."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Initiate a low-carbohydrate diet (Option A): While dietary management is important in diabetes, it is not the immediate initial step for a patient with significant hyperglycemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Start iron chelation therapy (Option B): Although iron chelation is critical for managing iron"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"overload, the immediate concern is the patient's hyperglycemia requiring insulin."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Schedule a follow-up for skin assessment (Option D): This is not appropriate as the patient needs urgent management for diabetes symptoms rather than a delayed assessment."}],"qi":[5015]},{"n":6,"q":"Which of the following genetic factors is most strongly associated with an increased risk of developing Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus (T1DM) in Caucasians?","o":[{"t":"HLA-B27"},{"t":"HLA-DR3/DR4"},{"t":"CD25 gene mutations"},{"t":"D) SH2B3 gene polymorphisms"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"HLA-DR3/DR4 haplotypes are strongly linked to increased susceptibility to T1DM in Caucasians."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aetiology and Pathogenesis of Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus (T1DM):"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"T1DM Overview:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Autoimmune disease with T-cell-mediated destruction of insulin-secreting b cells in pancreatic islets."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Involves insulitis (infiltration of islets by immune cells)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Combination of genetic predisposition and environmental triggers is key."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti-islet cell antibodies (ICA) are frequently found in Type 1 diabetes and are useful for diagnosing the condition, differentiating it from Type 2 diabetes, and identifying individuals at risk of developing Type 1 diabetes in the future."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Monozygotic twins: 30–50% concordance rate."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dizygotic twins: 6–10% concordance rate."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"First-degree relatives: 1:20 risk vs 1:300 in the general population."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HLA Region (Chromosome 6): DR3 and/or DR4 haplotypes linked to higher susceptibility in Caucasians."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DR3 and/or DR4 haplotypes linked to higher susceptibility in Caucasians."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other Genes: CD25, PTPN22, SH2B3, IL2RA, IL-10 involved in immune response. (Options C&D; ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DR3 and/or DR4 haplotypes linked to higher susceptibility in Caucasians."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Environmental Risk Factors for T1DM:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Risk Factor Details"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Viral Infections Mumps, Coxsackie B4, Rubella (in utero),"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Cytomegalovirus, Epstein–Barr"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Chemicals/Drug Nitrosamines (from smoked meats), coffee, BSA (bovine s serum albumin) in cow’s milk"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Dietary Hypoth Hygiene hypothesis (reduced exposure to eses microorganisms in early childhood might increase"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"autoimmune disease susceptibility.) Low vitamin D levels"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Natural History of Type 1 Diabetes:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Progressive b-cell destruction: Loss of first-phase insulin secretion. Glucose intolerance and undiagnosed diabetes before symptoms. Overt diabetes occurs when 80–90% of b-cell function is lost."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Loss of first-phase insulin secretion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Glucose intolerance and undiagnosed diabetes before symptoms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Overt diabetes occurs when 80–90% of b-cell function is lost."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Persistence of b Cells: Some b cells may persist even in long-standing T1DM cases. Variable rate of b-cell loss across individuals."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Some b cells may persist even in long-standing T1DM cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Variable rate of b-cell loss across individuals."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Loss of first-phase insulin secretion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Glucose intolerance and undiagnosed diabetes before symptoms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Overt diabetes occurs when 80–90% of b-cell function is lost."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Some b cells may persist even in long-standing T1DM cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Variable rate of b-cell loss across individuals."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"HLA-B27 (Option A): Associated with autoimmune conditions like ankylosing spondylitis, not T1DM."}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 23-year-old man with a family history of diabetes presents with a diagnosis of MODY confirmed by genetic testing. He exhibits stable mild hyperglycemia and has normal renal function. Which of the following subtypes of MODY is most likely responsible for his condition?","o":[{"t":"MODY1"},{"t":"MODY2"},{"t":"MODY3"},{"t":"MODY5"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MODY2 is characterized by mutations in the GCK gene, leading to stable mild hyperglycemia without requiring treatment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients with this subtype typically maintain normal renal function and do not experience significant progression of diabetes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Understanding MODY"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MODY (Maturity-Onset Diabetes of the Young) A subtype of diabetes occurring in individuals under 25 years. Non-insulin-dependent and hereditary with autosomal dominant inheritance. Family history often spans three or more generations. Caused by various genetic mutations with multiple subtypes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A subtype of diabetes occurring in individuals under 25 years."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Non-insulin-dependent and hereditary with autosomal dominant inheritance."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Family history often spans three or more generations."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Caused by various genetic mutations with multiple subtypes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A subtype of diabetes occurring in individuals under 25 years."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Non-insulin-dependent and hereditary with autosomal dominant inheritance."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Family history often spans three or more generations."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Caused by various genetic mutations with multiple subtypes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Subtypes and Genetic Mutations"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"MODY Subtypes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Subtype Gene Mutated Key Features"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"b-cell Glucose Sensing Subtype Gene Mutated Key Features"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"MODY2 GCK Mutations lead to altered glucose"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"sensing. Results in mild, stable hyperglycemia. High fasting glucose; normal postprandial response. Rarely requires treatment; low risk of complications."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"b-cell Transcriptional Regulation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"MODY1 (Option A ruled out) HNF4a Involves mutations affecting b-cell"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"function. Progressive diabetes requiring treatment."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"MODY3 (Option C ruled out) HNF1a Similar to MODY1 in progressive"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"nature. Responds well to sulfonylureas."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"MODY5 (Option D ruled out) HNF1b Associated with renal abnormalities"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(e.g., cystic disease). May cause genital malformations. Approximately 50% develop diabetes early in life."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Importance of Recognizing MODY"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tailored Treatment : Treatment can vary significantly based on genetic subtype. For example, MODY2 may not need medication, while HNF1a and HNF4a variants respond well to sulfonylureas."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prognosis and Complications : MODY2 generally has a favorable prognosis with low complication rates. Other subtypes may lead to progressive diabetes and higher complication risks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MODY2 generally has a favorable prognosis with low complication rates."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other subtypes may lead to progressive diabetes and higher complication risks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Genetic Counseling : Identifying MODY allows for targeted genetic counseling and testing for family members."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MODY2 generally has a favorable prognosis with low complication rates."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other subtypes may lead to progressive diabetes and higher complication risks."}]},{"n":8,"q":"A 55-year-old male with type 2 diabetes presents for a routine check-up. His medical history includes hypertension and smoking. Laboratory results show elevated LDL cholesterol levels. He is concerned about long-term complications of diabetes. Which of the following is the most important strategy to reduce his risk of macrovascular complications?","o":[{"t":"A) Tight glycaemic control only"},{"t":"Smoking cessation and statin therapy"},{"t":"Use of oral antihypertensives"},{"t":"Initiating low-dose aspirin therapy"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Smoking and dyslipidemia are major risk factors for macrovascular complications in diabetes, such as myocardial infarction and stroke. Statin therapy is essential to lower LDL cholesterol, while smoking cessation significantly reduces cardiovascular risk."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Macrovascular Complications of Diabetes Mellitus:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Major causes of morbidity and mortality in type 1 and type 2 diabetes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Involves large blood vessels, leading to:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Coronary circulation: Myocardial ischaemia/infarction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cerebral circulation: Transient ischaemic attack, stroke."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peripheral circulation: Claudication, ischaemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Key Points:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Excess mortality : Mainly due to myocardial infarction and stroke."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Accelerated atherosclerosis : Earlier onset and more severe in diabetics."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amplified risk factors : Smoking, hypertension, dyslipidemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Metabolic syndrome : Common in type 2 diabetes, with additional cardiovascular risk factors due to insulin resistance. Criteria for metabolic syndrome are Hypertension, High Fasting glucose, Elevated TG, Low HDL, Central obesity."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypertension,"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High Fasting glucose,"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated TG,"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low HDL,"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Central obesity."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypertension,"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High Fasting glucose,"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated TG,"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low HDL,"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Central obesity."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Statistics:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Condition Increased Rate in Diabetics"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cardiovascular death 1.7 times higher Condition Increased Rate in Diabetics"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Myocardial infarction 1.8 times greater"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Stroke hospitalisation 1.5 times higher"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Non-traumatic amputations 60% occur in diabetics"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"First cardiovascular event (type 1) 3 times higher in women, 2.3 times higher in men"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mechanisms linking hyperglycemia to macrovascular disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased vascular permeability : Due to high blood glucose."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lipoprotein deposition : Hyperinsulinemia and hypertension increase atherogenic lipoprotein deposits in large vessels."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Risk Management:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Glycaemic control : Reduces both microvascular and macrovascular complications."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blood pressure control : ACE inhibitors/ARBs are protective."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dyslipidemia management : Statins are recommended to lower cardiovascular risks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lifestyle modifications : Smoking cessation, weight management, regular physical activity are crucial."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tight glycaemic control only (Option A) : While glycaemic control is important, addressing other risk factors like smoking and dyslipidemia has a more immediate impact on reducing macrovascular complications."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Use of oral antihypertensives (Option C) : This alone may not be enough to reduce macrovascular risk, without addressing lipid levels and smoking, cardiovascular risk remains high."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Initiating low-dose aspirin therapy (Option D) : Aspirin may help prevent cardiovascular events in high-risk individuals, but smoking cessation and statin use are more crucial in managing overall risk."}]},{"n":9,"q":"A 55-year-old patient with poorly controlled diabetes mellitus presents with various complications. Which of the following conditions is not classified as a microvascular complication of diabetes mellitus?","o":[{"t":"Thickening of capillary basement membranes"},{"t":"Increased vascular permeability"},{"t":"Development of diabetic foot ulcers"},{"t":"Retinal ischemia develops over time leading to neovascularization"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Development of diabetic foot ulcers is not primarily classified as a microvascular complication; rather, it is a complication that can arise due to a combination of microvascular and macrovascular issues, including neuropathy and peripheral arterial disease."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diabetic Microvascular Complications"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetic microangiopathy affects small blood vessels in diabetes, leading to significant morbidity and mortality."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Key clinical syndromes include: Diabetic retinopathy Diabetic nephropathy Diabetic neuropathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetic retinopathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetic nephropathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetic neuropathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetic retinopathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetic nephropathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetic neuropathy"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prolonged hyperglycemia is believed to cause: Thickening of capillary basement membranes (Option A ruled out) Increased vascular permeability (Option B ruled out) Lipoprotein deposition in vessel walls (exacerbated by hyperinsulinemia and hypertension)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thickening of capillary basement membranes (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased vascular permeability (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lipoprotein deposition in vessel walls (exacerbated by hyperinsulinemia and hypertension)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thickening of capillary basement membranes (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased vascular permeability (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lipoprotein deposition in vessel walls (exacerbated by hyperinsulinemia and hypertension)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Key Microvascular Complications"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Complic Pathophysiology and Characteristics Management Strategies ation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Diabetic Prolonged hyperglycemia causes Regular eye examination, laser Retino biochemical changes that damage therapy, and additional treatments pathy retinal blood vessels. as needed."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Thickening of the retinal capillary Initial screening: basement membrane occurs. Type I DM - 3 to 5 years after Increased vascular permeability diagnosis causing microaneurysms. Type II DM - at the time of diagnosis Retinal ischemia develops over Screening is conducted annually, time, contributing to and if the results remain normal neovascularization. (Option D ruled over time, it can be done once every out) two years. Inflammatory mediators and oxidative stress"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Diabetic Early stages : hyperfiltration Aggressive blood pressure control Nephro Later stages: sustained proteinuria, (ACE inhibitors or ARBs), pathy nephrotic-range proteinuria, renal Lifestyle changes (diet, exercise),"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"failure. Possible renal replacement therapy Microalbuminuria predicts in later stages progression in type 1, less reliable in type 2."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Diabetic Axonal degeneration, schwann cell Pain management (anticonvulsants, Neurop thickening, demyelination. antidepressants, topical agents) and athy Classifications include symmetrical lifestyle modifications (glycemic"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"sensory polyneuropathy, control, exercise) to manage asymmetrical motor diabetic symptoms and slow progression neuropathy, mononeuropathy, and autonomic neuropathy."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Complic Pathophysiology and Characteristics Management Strategies ation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Diabetic Trauma : Minor injuries can lead to Patient Education: Educate patients Foot ulceration on daily foot inspections, proper Ulcers Neuropathy : Loss of sensation hygiene, appropriate footwear, and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"causes unawareness of injuries. prompt care for minor injuries. Structural foot changes , increasing Multidisciplinary Team Approach : pressure points and callus formation. Refer patients to a team including Charcot neuroarthropathy: diabetes specialists, podiatrists, deformities in foot bones and joints. vascular surgeons, and orthotists for Peripheral Vascular Disease: comprehensive care. Impaired blood flow hampers wound Debridement and Infection Control: healing and raises infection and Remove necrotic tissue and gangrene risk. (macrovascular administer timely antibiotic therapy complication) to manage infections. Callus Formation : Thickened skin Pressure Relief Strategies : Utilize areas can lead to necrosis beneath, custom insoles, orthotic footwear, resulting in ulceration. total contact casts, or aircast boots"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"to alleviate pressure on the ulcer. Revascularization and Amputation: Consider angioplasty or surgery for neuro-ischemic ulcers; perform amputations if severe infection or gangrene is present."}]},{"n":10,"q":"A 60-year-old man had an HbA1c of 6.1% during a routine check-up. He asks his physician about the best management strategy to prevent future progression of the disease. Which of the following is the most effective approach?","o":[{"t":"Starting medication immediately to control blood sugar levels"},{"t":"Undergoing bariatric surgery for weight loss"},{"t":"Implementing lifestyle modifications, including diet and exercise"},{"t":"Monitoring blood glucose levels without any other changes"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lifestyle modifications, such as a balanced diet and regular physical activity, are the most effective strategies for managing pre-diabetes (HbA1c: 5.7% - 6.5%) and preventing the onset of type 2 diabetes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Preventing Diabetes in Pre-Diabetic Individuals"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Key strategies for reducing the risk of progressing from pre-diabetes to type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular disease include:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Strategy Key Points"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Healthy Eating Focus on a balanced diet, limiting total and saturated"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"fats. Moderate carbohydrate intake (£ 50% of daily calories) with < 11% from added sugars. Consider low glycemic index (GI) foods (e.g., whole grains, legumes). Aim for at least five servings of fruits and vegetables daily. Consult a registered dietitian for personalized advice."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Weight Management Weight loss is crucial for overweight/obese individuals to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"improve glycemic control. Calorie restriction is essential for achieving weight loss."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Regular Physical Engage in at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity or Activity 75 minutes of vigorous-intensity exercise weekly."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Include muscle-strengthening exercises twice a week. Minimize sedentary time (>90 minutes of inactivity). Seek guidance for managing exercise-related hypoglycemia, especially in individuals with type 1 diabetes."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Alcohol Moderation Follow recommended alcohol intake guidelines to avoid"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"hypoglycemia. Consume alcohol with food to mitigate risks."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Smoking Cessation Quit smoking to lower the risk of cardiovascular disease"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"and other health issues."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Regular Monitoring Schedule regular checkups to monitor glucose levels,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"blood pressure, and lipid profiles. Manage cardiovascular risk factors aggressively. Consider early intervention with medications like metformin if necessary."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Starting medication immediately to control blood sugar levels (Option A) Medication may be necessary for some individuals but is not the first line of management for pre-diabetes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Undergoing bariatric surgery for weight loss (Option B) Bariatric surgery is a more invasive option typically reserved for individuals with obesity and related complications, not solely for pre-diabetes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Monitoring blood glucose levels without any other changes (Option D) Monitoring blood glucose levels alone without making lifestyle changes will not effectively prevent diabetes progression."}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 12-year-old girl presents with increased thirst, frequent urination, and fatigue over the past few weeks. Her parents mention that she has lost weight despite having a normal appetite. On examination, she appears dehydrated and has a fruity-smelling breath. Blood tests reveal a plasma glucose level of 350 mg/dL and a positive urine ketone test. Which of the following is true for this condition?","o":[{"t":"A) Auto-antibody present is anti-ICA"},{"t":"Increased fasting insulin levels"},{"t":"Polygenic cause"},{"t":"Due to recent hormonal changes"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This is characteristic of Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus, where autoimmune destruction of beta cells occurs, often accompanied by the presence of islet cell autoantibodies (anti-ICA)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type of DM Characteristics"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type 1 DM Pediatric onset"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Autoimmune destruction of >80% beta cells due to HLA association Leads to insulinopenia Auto-antibody: anti-ICA Risk of diabetic ketoacidosis (utilizes ketone bodies for energy)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type 2 DM Relative insulin resistance"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Increased fasting insulin levels (radioimmune insulin levels) (Option B) Polygenic cause (Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"MODY (Maturity Onset Diabetes of the Monogenic form Young) Autosomal dominant inheritance pattern"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Expressed in every generation At least 2 consecutive generations with DM history It resembles Type 2 diabetes but usually occurs in younger individuals."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (Option D) Defined as:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"1. Plasma glucose > 140 mg/dL after 75g of glucose (WHO criteria) 2. After 100g glucose (Carpenter/Coustan diagnostic criteria - ADA criteria): 1 hr later, plasma glucose ‡ 180 mg/dL 2 hr later, plasma glucose ‡ 155 mg/dL 3 hr later, plasma glucose ‡ 140 mg/dL"}]},{"n":12,"q":"A 50-year-old woman presents for a routine check-up. She is overweight but has no family history of diabetes or hypertension. According to the American Diabetes Association (ADA) guidelines, what is the most appropriate screening recommendation for her?","o":[{"t":"No screening is necessary until age 60."},{"t":"Screen now and re-screen every 3 years if normal."},{"t":"Screen every year regardless of results."},{"t":"Screen only if symptoms of diabetes appear."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The American Diabetes Association recommends that adults aged 45 and older undergo screening for diabetes, regardless of risk factors."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Since the patient is 50 years old, she should be screened now, with rescreening every 3 years if the results are normal, ensuring early detection and intervention if necessary."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diabetes Mellitus Screening Recommendations"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"General Population"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Adults ‡45 years : Begin screening at 45, even without risk factors; repeat every 3 years if results are normal."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Overweight/Obese Adults : Screen earlier if they have risk factors like family history, hypertension, or belong to high-risk ethnic groups."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"High-Risk Groups"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prediabetes : Screen annually to monitor progression."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High-Risk Ethnicities : Screen at a younger age (e.g., African Americans, Latinos, Native Americans, Asian Americans, Pacific Islanders)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gestational Diabetes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pregnant Women : Universal screening is recommended in the second trimester or earlier if risk factors exist."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"No screening is necessary until age 60 (Option A) : Screening should begin at age 45 regardless of other factors."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Screen every year regardless of results (Option C) : Annual screening is only recommended for those with prediabetes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Screen only if symptoms of diabetes appear (Option D) : Screening should not wait for symptoms to appear, especially for at-risk individuals."}]},{"n":13,"q":"A glucagon-stimulated C-peptide test is performed on a patient receiving exogenous insulin therapy. Which of the following findings is most likely in a patient with long-standing type 1 diabetes?","o":[{"t":"Elevated serum C-peptide levels after glucagon stimulation"},{"t":"Decreased or absent serum C-peptide levels"},{"t":"Normal C-peptide levels but reduced blood glucose response"},{"t":"Elevated C-peptide levels with low insulin resistance"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In long-standing type 1 diabetes, there is a significant loss of b-cell function, resulting in low or undetectable C-peptide levels, even after stimulation. This differentiates it from type 2 diabetes, where C-peptide levels are typically higher."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Glucagon-Stimulated C-Peptide Test"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Purpose : Assesses endogenous insulin production, especially in patients on exogenous insulin."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"C-Peptide"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"C-peptide : A byproduct formed when insulin is cleaved from pro-insulin in pancreatic b cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Structure : Pro-insulin = a-chain + b-chain + C-peptide. b-cell enzymes cleave C-peptide, leaving active insulin (a and b chains) and free C-peptide."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"b-cell enzymes cleave C-peptide, leaving active insulin (a and b chains) and free C-peptide."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Detection : Measured via immunoassays in blood or urine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"b-cell enzymes cleave C-peptide, leaving active insulin (a and b chains) and free C-peptide."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Uses of Glucagon-Stimulated C-Peptide Test"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Differentiates diabetes types : Low C-peptide fi Long-standing type 1 diabetes. (Option B) High C-peptide fi Severe insulin resistance. (Options A & D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low C-peptide fi Long-standing type 1 diabetes. (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High C-peptide fi Severe insulin resistance. (Options A & D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Measures endogenous insulin in patients on exogenous insulin : Synthetic insulin lacks C-peptide, so serum C-peptide helps evaluate pancreatic insulin production."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Synthetic insulin lacks C-peptide, so serum C-peptide helps evaluate pancreatic insulin production."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aids in diagnosing hypoglycemia : Helps detect spontaneous hypoglycemia causes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low C-peptide fi Long-standing type 1 diabetes. (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High C-peptide fi Severe insulin resistance. (Options A & D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Synthetic insulin lacks C-peptide, so serum C-peptide helps evaluate pancreatic insulin production."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Procedure for the Glucagon-Stimulated C-Peptide Test"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Step Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Baseline sample Draw blood to measure the initial C-peptide level."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Glucagon injection Inject glucagon to stimulate insulin production."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Follow-up samples Take blood samples at intervals (e.g., 30 & 60 minutes)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"post-injection."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Analysis : Comparing C-peptide levels before and after stimulation helps assess pancreatic beta-cell function."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Normal C-peptide levels but reduced blood glucose response (Option C): The glucagon-stimulated test primarily measures C-peptide to evaluate endogenous insulin production, not blood glucose directly."}]},{"n":14,"q":"A 56-year-old male with type 2 diabetes presents for a follow-up after recent medication changes. His physician wants to assess how well his blood glucose levels have been controlled over the past few weeks. Which of the following tests is the most appropriate for short-term monitoring of his glycemic control?","o":[{"t":"HbA1c"},{"t":"Fructosamine"},{"t":"Fasting Blood Glucose"},{"t":"Oral Glucose Tolerance Test (OGTT)"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fructosamine is the best choice for short-term glycemic control monitoring because it reflects average blood glucose levels over the past 2-3 weeks , allowing for timely adjustments in diabetes management."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is crucial when monitoring changes after medication adjustments, unlike HbA1c, which indicates longer-term control over 2-3 months."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fructosamine Testing:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Significance"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fructosamine : Formed by glucose binding to serum proteins (mainly albumin)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Duration of Reflection : Reflects average glucose levels over the last 2-3 weeks ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Advantages"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Short-Term Monitoring : Ideal for assessing recent glycemic changes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Unaffected by Hemoglobin Variants : Useful in cases like sickle cell disease or anaemia where HbA1c may be unreliable."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fast & Convenient : No fasting is required; serum/plasma samples are used."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Reference Ranges"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Population Fructosamine Levels (µmol/L)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Non-diabetic 200–285"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diabetic (poor control) Elevated above 285"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Comparison to HbA1c : HbA1c reflects glucose over 2-3 months . (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Comparison to Fasting, Postprandial Plasma Glucose and OGTT: Provide only single-time-point results. (Options C & D ruled out)"}]},{"n":15,"q":"A 45-year-old obese woman with no acute symptoms presents for routine screening. Given her family history of diabetes, which diagnostic test would provide the most reliable indication of her glycemic status and aid in the early detection of diabetes?","o":[{"t":"Fasting Plasma Glucose (FPG)"},{"t":"Oral Glucose Tolerance Test (OGTT)"},{"t":"Random Plasma Glucose (RPG)"},{"t":"Glycated Hemoglobin (HbA1c)"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Glycated Hemoglobin (HbA1c) effectively assesses overall glycemic control and is suitable for diagnosing diabetes in asymptomatic individuals."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It provides a more comprehensive view of glucose levels compared to other tests, with a diagnostic threshold of ‡ 48 mmol/mol (6.5%), making it ideal for early detection, particularly in patients with risk factors like obesity and family history."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HbA1c prediction: < 5.7% : Normal 5.7% - 6.5% : Pre-diabetic > 6.5% : Diabetic"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"< 5.7% : Normal"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"5.7% - 6.5% : Pre-diabetic"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"> 6.5% : Diabetic"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"< 5.7% : Normal"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"5.7% - 6.5% : Pre-diabetic"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"> 6.5% : Diabetic"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigating and Diagnosing Diabetes Mellitus"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Blood Glucose Tests:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fasting Plasma Glucose (FPG) (Option A ruled out) Measures glucose after an overnight fast. Diagnostic threshold: ‡ 126 mg/dL (7.0 mmol/L). Simple, cost-effective, and widely available."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Measures glucose after an overnight fast."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic threshold: ‡ 126 mg/dL (7.0 mmol/L)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Simple, cost-effective, and widely available."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Random Plasma Glucose (RPG) (Option C ruled out) Measures glucose at any time, irrespective of meals. Diagnostic threshold: ‡ 200 mg/dL (11.1 mmol/L). Useful for immediate diagnosis in symptomatic patients."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Measures glucose at any time, irrespective of meals."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic threshold: ‡ 200 mg/dL (11.1 mmol/L)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Useful for immediate diagnosis in symptomatic patients."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral Glucose Tolerance Test (OGTT) (Option B ruled out) Fasting glucose was measured, followed by a 75 g glucose drink, then re-measured after 2 hours. Diagnostic threshold: ‡ 200 mg/dL (11.1 mmol/L). More time-consuming; identifies impaired glucose tolerance."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fasting glucose was measured, followed by a 75 g glucose drink, then re-measured after 2 hours."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic threshold: ‡ 200 mg/dL (11.1 mmol/L)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"More time-consuming; identifies impaired glucose tolerance."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Measures glucose after an overnight fast."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic threshold: ‡ 126 mg/dL (7.0 mmol/L)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Simple, cost-effective, and widely available."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Measures glucose at any time, irrespective of meals."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic threshold: ‡ 200 mg/dL (11.1 mmol/L)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Useful for immediate diagnosis in symptomatic patients."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fasting glucose was measured, followed by a 75 g glucose drink, then re-measured after 2 hours."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic threshold: ‡ 200 mg/dL (11.1 mmol/L)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"More time-consuming; identifies impaired glucose tolerance."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Important Considerations for Blood Glucose Testing"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Venous plasma values are more reliable for diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Capillary blood glucose (finger prick) results are not suitable for diagnosis due to variability."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Glycated Hemoglobin (HbA1c): An Emerging Standard (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reflects average glucose levels over 2-3 months."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic threshold: ‡ 48 mmol/mol (6.5%) as per WHO."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Not recommended for: Suspected type 1 diabetes or recent hyperglycemia. Pregnancy due to increased red blood cell turnover."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Suspected type 1 diabetes or recent hyperglycemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pregnancy due to increased red blood cell turnover."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Suspected type 1 diabetes or recent hyperglycemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pregnancy due to increased red blood cell turnover."}]},{"n":16,"q":"A 60-year-old male with a history of hypertension presents for a routine check-up. He has no symptoms but is concerned about his blood sugar levels. His lab results show: Fasting plasma glucose: 128 mg/dL (7.1 mmol/L) HbA1c: 6.7% What is the next best step?","o":[{"t":"Repeat fasting plasma glucose for confirmation"},{"t":"Perform an oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT)"},{"t":"Start lifestyle modifications and monitor for pre-diabetes"},{"t":"Diagnose diabetes mellitus and initiate treatment"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In asymptomatic patients, a diagnosis of diabetes mellitus requires confirmation with a repeat fasting plasma glucose test, as a single elevated reading (‡ 126 mg/dL) is not sufficient for diagnosis without corroboration."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This ensures accuracy and reduces the risk of false positives, guiding appropriate management."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosing Diabetes Mellitus Diabetes Mellitus"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis is based on confirming hyperglycemia through the following methods:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Test Diagnostic Threshold for Diabetes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Random plasma glucose ‡ 200 mg/dL"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Fasting plasma glucose ‡ 126 mg/dL"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Oral glucose tolerance test ‡ 200 mg/dL after 2 hours with 75 g glucose. (OGTT) OGTT is not necessary if fasting glucose is already in"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"the diabetic range. (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c) < 5.7% : Normal"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"5.7% - 6.5% : Pre-diabetic > 6.5% : Diabetic"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Asymptomatic patients : Two positive tests (same method) are required for confirmation. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HbA1c Limitation : Not suitable for recent-onset diabetes (e.g., type 1 diabetes or severe hyperglycemia) as it reflects the past 2-3 months of glycemic control."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pre-diabetes:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pre-diabetes is characterized by elevated glucose levels but not meeting diabetic thresholds."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Type Blood Glucose Range"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Impaired fasting glucose 110–125 mg/dL (6.1–6.9 mmol/L) (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Impaired glucose tolerance Fasting < 126 mg/dL + 2-hour OGTT: 140–200 mg/dL"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(7.8–11.1 mmol/L)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pre-diabetes indicates a higher risk of progression to diabetes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Other Investigations:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigation Purpose"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Islet Autoantibodies Indicates type 1 diabetes (autoimmune destruction)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"C-peptide Measurement Differentiates type 1 (low C-peptide) vs. severe insulin"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"resistance (high C-peptide)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Urine Protein Testing Detects microalbuminuria for nephropathy and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cardiovascular risk."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lipid Profile Identifies dyslipidemia, a common comorbidity in"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"diabetes."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigation Purpose"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Liver and Thyroid Tests Detects abnormalities related to diabetes complications."}]},{"n":17,"q":"Which of the following drugs is most likely to cause drug-induced diabetes by impairing insulin secretion from the pancreas?","o":[{"t":"Glucocorticoids"},{"t":"Thiazide diuretics"},{"t":"Phenytoin"},{"t":"b-lactam antibiotics"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thiazide diuretics are known to impair insulin secretion from the pancreas (possibly secondary to the hypokalemia induced by the thiazide diuretics), leading to increased blood glucose levels and a higher risk of developing diabetes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Drugs and Conditions Associated with Diabetes or Hyperglycemia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Drugs That Can Cause Diabetes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Drug Class Mechanism Key Note"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Glucocorticoids Increase insulin resistance (Option Can cause temporary or type 2"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"A ruled out) diabetes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Thiazide diuretics Impair insulin secretion from the Used to treat hypertension"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"pancreas (Option B)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Phenytoin Possible liver-related mechanism Anticonvulsant medication"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Drugs Affecting Glucose Levels but Not Directly Causing Diabetes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Drug Effect"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"b-lactam antibiotics Interfere with urine glucose tests (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Levodopa May cause false-positive glucose results"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Salicylates Affect glucose measurements in urine"}]},{"n":18,"q":"A 45-year-old man presents with complaints of progressive changes in his appearance over the past few years. He notes that his shoes no longer fit, and his wedding ring feels tight on his finger. His wife has remarked that he seems to have developed more pronounced facial features. He also reports excessive sweating and occasional headaches. On examination, there is noticeable frontal bossing. Which of the following complications is most likely associated with his condition?","o":[{"t":"A) Microglossia"},{"t":"B) Hypotension"},{"t":"Diabetes mellitus"},{"t":"D) High-pitched voice"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This case describes a 45-year-old man exhibiting signs of acromegaly, including changes in facial features and excessive sweating ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Insulin resistance is a common complication due to elevated growth hormone levels, leading to impaired glucose metabolism and an increased risk of developing diabetes."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Category Causes of Diabetes Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pancreati Damage to the exocrine component Causes diarrhea and steatorrhea c Damage of the pancreas due to malabsorption and DM"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Cystic fibrosis: common cause manifestations."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Chronic Insulinopenia occurs due to reduced Lack of insulin leads to elevated pancreatiti beta cell mass. blood sugar s"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Endocrino Acromegaly Increased growth hormone (GH) pathies levels lead to insulin resistance."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pheochro Increased norepinephrine or mocytoma epinephrine levels cause elevated"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"blood sugar, palpitations, and sweating."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cushing's Grossly elevated cortisol levels lead Syndrome to insulin resistance and DM."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Medicatio Steroids Long-term use can induce insulin ns resistance."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Immunom mTOR inhibitors and calcineurin odulators inhibitors can affect glucose"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"metabolism"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Thiazides Hydrochlorothiazide may cause"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"elevated HbA1c values, leading to impaired glucose tolerance."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Propranol It can affect glucose metabolism, ol and it is used for migraine"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"prophylaxis and anxiety management."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Antipsych Some antipsychotic medications otics can lead to weight gain and insulin"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"resistance."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Microglossia (Option A): acromegaly typically causes macroglossia (enlarged tongue)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypotension (Option B): is not typical in acromegaly, as patients usually have hypertension."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"High-pitched voice (Option D): this is incorrect because acromegaly often results in a deeper voice due to changes in the larynx."}]},{"n":19,"q":"Which of the following statements about Type 1.5 Diabetes Mellitus is incorrect?","o":[{"t":"A) Characterized by autoimmune destruction of beta cells"},{"t":"Patients are typically diagnosed in childhood"},{"t":"It is often misdiagnosed as Type 2 DM"},{"t":"D) C-peptide levels decline more slowly compared to Type 1 DM"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type 1.5 DM or Latent Autoimmune Diabetes in Adults (LADA) is usually diagnosed in adults, often in their 20s to 30s, distinguishing it from Type 1 DM which commonly presents in childhood. It characteristics of both Type 1 and Type 2 diabetes, which is why it is referred to as Type 1.5."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type 1.5 DM (LADA)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Etiology: Similar to Type 1 DM due to autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Similar to Type 1 DM due to autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Age-wise Presentation: Resembles Type 2 DM because the beta cell destruction is gradual. Typically diagnosed in adults, often in their 20s to 30s."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Resembles Type 2 DM because the beta cell destruction is gradual."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Typically diagnosed in adults, often in their 20s to 30s."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antibodies: Presence of anti-GAD (glutamic acid decarboxylase) antibodies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presence of anti-GAD (glutamic acid decarboxylase) antibodies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Misdiagnosis: Frequently misdiagnosed as Type 2 DM due to adult onset."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Frequently misdiagnosed as Type 2 DM due to adult onset."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"C-Peptide Levels: Decrease slowly compared to Type 1 DM, reflecting gradual beta cell loss."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decrease slowly compared to Type 1 DM, reflecting gradual beta cell loss."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment: It is initially managed similar to Type 2 diabetes, but as the progression of the condition is comparatively early, insulin therapy may eventually become necessary."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is initially managed similar to Type 2 diabetes, but as the progression of the condition is comparatively early, insulin therapy may eventually become necessary."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Similar to Type 1 DM due to autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Resembles Type 2 DM because the beta cell destruction is gradual."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Typically diagnosed in adults, often in their 20s to 30s."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presence of anti-GAD (glutamic acid decarboxylase) antibodies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Frequently misdiagnosed as Type 2 DM due to adult onset."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decrease slowly compared to Type 1 DM, reflecting gradual beta cell loss."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is initially managed similar to Type 2 diabetes, but as the progression of the condition is comparatively early, insulin therapy may eventually become necessary."}]}]);
